"The map is the separation-of-powers frame with the agencies caught inside it. Two tall pillars bound the course: on the left Congress (the statute is made at the top, read through Katzmann's methods beneath, and handed over to agencies at the foot), on the right the courts (how they read statutes at the top, judicial review in the middle level with the agencies it polices, standing as the foundation). Between the pillars sits the executive as two stacked rows: the President and the officers above (appointments nearest Congress, presidential control nearest the courts, removal in the middle where the two political branches fight over independence), and directly beneath them the agencies doing their work (adjudication under the appointments cases about its judges, rulemaking in the centre, notice-and-comment against the courts), so the vertical axis inside the executive literally reads as control from above. The horizontal axis is the life of a statute, statute to agency to court, and the syllabus survives as a reading path (steps 1-2 down the left pillar, 3-5 across the top row, the agencies row, 9-11 down the right pillar). Heavily connected pairs are adjacent: the interpretation continuation under its parent (44 ribbons), judicial review level with the agencies row and touching notice-and-comment rulemaking (10), presidential control against judicial review, appointments against removal. Spine lines mark the three inter-branch tensions (Congress vs President over delegation and removal, President vs courts, courts reading statutes with Congress's tools) and the flows down and across. The bookkeeping region sits alone in the bottom margin under the agencies, outside every zone."